<h2 id="who">1. Who is responsible for your data</h2>

<p>
  The data controller for this website is <strong>Stefanos Tzortzoglou</strong>, a private individual based in
  Uppsala, Sweden. This site is a personal portfolio, not a business: nothing is sold here and no company is behind
  it.
</p>
<p>
  Contact for any privacy question or request:
  <a href="mailto:stefanos@tzortzoglou.eu">stefanos@tzortzoglou.eu</a>.
</p>
<p>
  Because the controller is established in Sweden, this policy is governed by the EU General Data Protection Regulation
  (GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679) and the Swedish Data Protection Act
  (<span lang="sv">lag (2018:218) med kompletterande bestämmelser till EU:s dataskyddsförordning</span>).
</p>

<h2 id="browsing">2. If you only browse the site</h2>

<p>You do not need an account to read anything on the public pages. You are asked for information only if you
  choose to send it: by creating an account (<a href="#account">section 3</a>) or by using the contact form
  (<a href="#contact">section 4</a>).
  Three things still happen automatically, because that is how the web works:</p>

<h3 id="hosting-logs">2.1 Hosting logs</h3>
<p>
  The site is hosted on <strong>GitHub Pages</strong> (GitHub, Inc., United States). To deliver and protect the pages,
  GitHub processes technical connection data including your <strong>IP address</strong>, the requested URL, and your
  browser's user-agent string. I have no access to these logs and cannot read them.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> serving the site and protecting it from abuse.</li>
  <li><strong>Legal basis:</strong> legitimate interests, Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR — running a functioning, secure website.</li>
  <li><strong>Transfer outside the EEA:</strong> yes, to the United States, under GitHub's Data Protection Agreement and
    the EU Standard Contractual Clauses. See
    <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub's privacy statement</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="analytics">2.2 Analytics</h3>
<p>
  <strong>This section reversed on 18 September 2026.</strong> Until that day a counted page view
  carried no identifier, and this section said so in those words. A counted page view now carries a
  visitor number, the same for your browser all day and different tomorrow.
</p>
<p>
  If you accept the banner, page views are counted by a small endpoint on <strong>api.tzortzoglou.eu</strong>, the
  same application that receives the contact form, running on a virtual private server rented in my own name. No
  analytics company is involved and nothing is loaded from one: your browser sends one short message to my own server,
  which sends nothing back.
</p>
<p>
  The number is a 64-bit hash of four things: a secret my server holds, your IP address, your browser's
  User-Agent string, and which of my two sites you were on. A new secret replaces the old one every 24 hours, and the
  old one is destroyed the moment the new one is made, so yesterday's numbers cannot be recomputed from anything that
  still exists. Your IP address and your User-Agent go into the hash and are not written down.
</p>
<p>
  Two page views from the same browser on the same day carry the same number, so they are linked to each other.
  Tomorrow that browser gets a different number, and the secret that would relink the two days is gone. Anonymity
  would be a larger claim and I am not making it. Within a single day, one browser's page views can be picked out as
  a group.
</p>
<p>
  That makes a counted page view pseudonymous data, and under the GDPR pseudonymous data is personal data
  (Recital 26), so the rights in <a href="#rights">section 6</a> apply to it. Consent remains the legal basis for it
  on this public site, Art. 6(1)(a).
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>What a counted page view records:</strong> the number, the path of the page (without its query string
    or fragment, so anything sitting in this site's own address bar stays in your browser), which of my two sites it
    was, the <em>host</em> of the page that linked you here (<code>www.linkedin.com</code>, not the full address), the
    time, and four coarse facts about your browser: its name (Firefox, Safari), its operating system (Windows,
    Android), whether it is a phone, a tablet or a desktop, and which of a handful of width brackets its window falls
    into. The window width is the one new thing your browser sends, and my server keeps only which bracket it fell
    into.</li>
  <li><strong>What it uses and discards:</strong> your IP address and the full User-Agent string. Both are inputs to
    the hash and neither is written to the page view.</li>
  <li><strong>What it still does not record:</strong> no cookie, no fingerprint read off your hardware, fonts or
    canvas, and never the full referring URL. The full URL is the one place a search term can ride: your browser hands
    the referring address over in full, as it does to every site you click through to, and my server reduces it to its
    host and discards the rest before anything is written. The trimming is real, but it happens on arrival, not before
    sending.</li>
  <li><strong>What is refused outright:</strong> a request whose User-Agent identifies it as a bot or a crawler is not
    counted at all.</li>
  <li><strong>Where the request goes:</strong> to that server, which I run, so unlike the hosting logs in
    <a href="#hosting-logs">section 2.1</a>, this one connection is to me, not to GitHub. Your IP address arrives
    with it, because every request carries one, and it is used twice, both times in memory: as the key a rate limiter
    counts against, so that the endpoint cannot be hammered, and as one of the four inputs to the hash. It is never
    written to the page view and never stored.</li>
  <li><strong>If you ask me to find what I hold about you:</strong> the answer here used to be that there was nothing
    to find. Within the current day, a row could in principle be matched to you: someone with your IP address and your
    User-Agent could recompute the hash while that day's secret still exists and see which rows come out equal. Once
    the secret is discarded that is no longer possible, and the rows from earlier days are numbers with nothing left
    to compare them against.</li>
  <li><strong>How long a counted page view is kept:</strong> the number is erased from the row 30 days after the page
    view. The row stays and the counts stay; the field that ties one view to another is emptied. The rest of the row
    has no scheduled deletion, and if the table ever grows to the point of mattering, rows older than 24 months will
    be deleted and this paragraph will say so.</li>
  <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> understanding, in aggregate, which pages people find useful, and how many separate
    browsers a day's views came from rather than only how many views there were.</li>
  <li><strong>Legal basis:</strong> your consent, Art. 6(1)(a) GDPR. Nothing is counted at all unless you accept it
    on the banner shown on your first visit, and you can withdraw that consent at any time, as easily as you gave it,
    from the <a href="/cookies/">Cookies &amp; Local Storage</a> page (Art. 7(3)).</li>
  <li><strong>If you decline or ignore the banner:</strong> nothing is sent, nothing is counted, and the site works
    exactly the same.</li>
  <li><strong>Country, which is not here yet:</strong> I intend to store a two-letter country code with each page
    view. The lookup will read a MaxMind database file stored on my own server, and the two letters are all that
    would be stored. No country is recorded today, and this section will be rewritten before the first one is.</li>
  <li><strong>Before September 2026</strong> this was done by <strong>GoatCounter</strong>, a third-party service.
    Nothing is sent there any more; the figures it gathered up to the switchover are frozen,
    and still readable at
    <a href="https://steftzor.goatcounter.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">steftzor.goatcounter.com</a>.
  </li>
</ul>

<h3>2.3 Third-party files loaded by your browser</h3>
<p>
  Some files are loaded directly from other providers, not from this domain. When your browser fetches those
  files, it connects to those providers directly, and they necessarily receive your
  <strong>IP address</strong>, your user-agent, and the page you were on. This happens on page load, before you
  interact with anything. On these public pages that is now one provider on one page: the rendered GitHub statistics
  images on the <a href="/code/">Code</a> page. The typeface and every icon are served from this domain. The full list is in the
  <a href="/cookies/">Cookies &amp; Local Storage</a> page.
</p>
<p>
  I do not control what those providers do with that connection data. If you would rather not connect to them, a
  content-blocking browser extension will prevent the requests; the site remains readable, though those statistics
  images will not render. The application behind the sign-in loads more from elsewhere than these
  pages do, including a map, which <a href="#map-tiles">section 3.3</a> is about.
</p>

<h2 id="account">3. If you create an account</h2>

<p>
